---
// Public URL supplied to app stores for account-deletion instructions.
import PolicyPage from "../components/PolicyPage.astro";

const UPDATED = "August 12, 2026";
const CONTACT_EMAIL = "feedback@siloserver.org";
---

<PolicyPage
  title="delete a Silo account"
  description="How Silo Media Player users request deletion of an account and associated data from the operator of their self-hosted Silo server."
  eyebrow="legal / 02"
  heading="Delete a Silo account"
  meta={`Last updated ${UPDATED}`}
>
  <p class="policy__lede">
    Silo Media Player does not provide or host centralized user accounts. An
    account created in the app belongs to the self-hosted Silo server selected
    by the user. That server's operator—not the Silo project—controls the
    account and its associated data.
  </p>

  <h2>Request deletion from your server operator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Identify the person or organization that gave you the Silo server
      address, invitation, or login. That is normally the server operator.
    </li>
    <li>
      Contact that operator and ask them to delete your Silo user account and
      associated data.
    </li>
    <li>
      Give the operator the username or email address used on that server so
      they can locate the correct account. Share the server address and account
      details only with that operator—not with the Silo project.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The Silo project cannot identify the operator of an arbitrary self-hosted
    server, access its administration tools, verify its users, or delete data
    from it.
  </p>

  <h2>Instructions for a Silo server administrator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Sign in to the self-hosted Silo web application as an administrator.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Administration → Users</strong>.</li>
    <li>Select the requesting user and choose <strong>Delete user</strong>.</li>
    <li>Confirm the permanent deletion.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Account deletion is performed by the self-hosted server. It does not send
    account information or a deletion request to the Silo project.
  </p>

  <h2>Data deleted from the live server</h2>
  <p>
    Deleting a user removes the account from the live Silo database and
    invalidates its active sessions. Associated user-scoped records are also
    deleted, including, where present:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>profiles and profile access assignments;</li>
    <li>authentication sessions, API keys, and registered device records;</li>
    <li>settings and preferences;</li>
    <li>watch history, playback progress, ratings, favorites, and watchlist;</li>
    <li>personal collections and related user-created state; and</li>
    <li>user-scoped download, notification, request, and synchronization state.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Data that may be kept</h2>
  <p>
    A server operator may retain backups, security logs, audit records, or
    information required by law or legitimate security needs. The operator
    determines those retention periods and is responsible for explaining them
    to the user. Some shared library metadata or administrative records may
    remain without being associated with the deleted account.
  </p>

  <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Removing a server from Silo Media Player, signing out, clearing the app's
    storage, or uninstalling the app removes the applicable local credentials,
    caches, settings, and downloaded media. These actions do not delete the
    account held by the self-hosted server; the server operator must still
    complete the steps above.
  </p>

  <h2>Silo-managed diagnostics and push delivery</h2>
  <p>
    Optional Silo-managed diagnostic reports are not linked to an account on a
    self-hosted server, so deleting that account does not identify or delete a
    previously sent report. Diagnostic reports expire within 30 days. To
    request earlier report deletion, email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and include the
    report reference shown in the app.
  </p>
  <p>
    The content-free Silo push relay does not hold self-hosted account records.
    Revoking a device or deleting the user on the self-hosted server stops that
    server from sending future pushes for the deleted account.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about these instructions may be sent to
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. Do not send your
    password, authentication token, private server address, or other account
    credentials. Silo cannot forward a deletion request to an unknown
    self-hosted server operator.
  </p>
</PolicyPage>
